---
type: Diagnostic
description: Synthetic reproduction establishes a slash-template parser defect and an absent MCP import tool.
title: ImportSession template parser consumes explanatory prose
timestamp: 2026-10-17
tags: [import-session, regression, mcp, synthetic-fixtures]
---

# Established findings

1. ImportSession is a generated slash command, NOT a registered MCP tool.
   `ciel_runtime_support/slash_command_assets.py:146-158` emits the marker;
   `runtime_asset_assembly.py:103-105,136-140` registers command assets.
   `channel_mcp_http_controller.py:189-199` routes tools/list and tools/call;
   `channel_mcp_tools.py:40-341,406` has no import schema/dispatch branch and returns
   Unknown ciel-runtime-router tool. Synthetic real controller calls verified all
   three spellings importsession, ImportSession, import_session. No success claim
   is made for importing through MCP; that feature remains absent.
2. Actual shipped command has explanatory prose after Arguments, separated by a
   blank line (`slash_command_assets.py:155-157`). The HEAD parser captures every
   remaining line (`request_shortcuts.py:224-227` before patch), passes it through
   split_import_session_arguments, whose literal `transcript` path flag
   (`request_shortcuts.py:194-195`) resets the path to `format`, then appends the
   remaining prose. The observed path is `format and must be `Codex` or `Claude`.`.
   Source resolution therefore fails even with a valid synthetic transcript.
   Both Claude-to-Codex and Codex-to-Claude directions reproduce this failure.
3. Narrow fix: `request_shortcuts.py:225-228` stops Arguments at the paragraph
   boundary after collected arguments. Existing single-line and multiline
   arguments and quoted paths remain tested. No MCP registration, policy, or
   transcript-format architecture change was attempted.

# Source resolution, formats, truncation and permissions

- `session_import.py:20-26` accepts only Claude/Codex aliases, not Muse.
  `:46-58` discovers ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl or CODEX_HOME/sessions/**/*.jsonl;
  `:76-98` resolves latest/directory/local-file paths and rejects URLs. A session
  ID alone is treated as a path, not looked up by ID. Latest is global per root,
  not restricted to the current workspace.
- Muse discovery DOES exist in the separate channel transcript repository
  (`channel_transcript_repository.py:283-300`, */*/*/*/session.jsonl), but it is not
  wired into ImportSession. No actual Muse conversation was read; Muse source
  format/record decoding is NOT validated here. Passing Muse produces Usage.
- `session_import.py:226-232` intentionally blocks Claude-to-Claude imports.
  This is existing policy, not changed as a supposed bug.
- `session_import.py:101-141` reads only the byte tail and then trims characters.
  Synthetic tests verify warning metadata and latest complete record retention.
  A single record larger than the byte budget produces partial raw JSON with
  jsonl=false, records=0, yet nonempty content. This is a separate observed
  limitation, not repaired here.
- `session_import.py:233-242` calls resolve outside its read exception handler.
  Synthetic injected read PermissionError is correctly reported. Real Windows
  ACL denial and resolve-time permission failures were not exercised; do not
  claim permissions as the report's cause.
- Claude message and Codex response_item/event_msg synthetic content is decoded
  by `session_import.py:188-206` using `channel_transcript.py:220-234,310+`.

# Fail-before / pass-after

Run from the repository with Python 3.11:

    python -I -X utf8 docs/journal/2026/10/17/diagnostics/import-session/run_focused.py --baseline
    python -I -X utf8 docs/journal/2026/10/17/diagnostics/import-session/run_focused.py

The runner strips inherited CIEL_RUNTIME_* variables, uses the project's
isolate_runtime_state helper, and selects four test files only. Baseline loads
HEAD's original parser IN MEMORY via git show; no working file is reverted.

- before.txt: 23 tests; 12 assertion failures including subtests; exit 1.
  Actual ImportSessionHttpController.handle returns transcript-not-found for
  both synthetic cross-runtime fixtures despite their files existing.
- after.txt: same 23 tests pass; exit 0. The real import controller reads the
  synthetic files and returns ImportSession completed with synthetic content.
- MCP test invokes real ChannelMcpHttpController.post and the real dispatcher;
  response writer is captured, not a real HTTP socket. All import tool names
  remain errors before AND after. This is real handler invocation in-process,
  not a live router/client E2E, and not a successful MCP import test.
- Other tests are repository/parser unit tests; read PermissionError is injected.
- No UI change. CLI/server text logs are the verification evidence; no browser
  screenshot was applicable or taken. No live config/session, remote machine,
  runtime launch, install, commit, push, or unrelated TLS file was touched.

# Scope and uncertainty

The exact reported failed invocation and error were not supplied. Established:
a literal MCP call cannot succeed because no tool is registered, and the shipped
slash command has a deterministic parser defect. This is NOT proof that a new
October TLS change caused this issue. Blame traces the template to d19784be and
the extracted parser to 4c48edfc (July 19); introduction before extraction has
not been determined. Native/direct paths bypassing marker interception and Muse
source support require separate product decisions, not speculative expansion.

# Changes

- ciel_runtime_support/request_shortcuts.py: four added lines only.
- tests/test_request_shortcuts.py: generated-template/paragraph regressions.
- tests/test_session_import_regression.py: synthetic controller/repository tests.
- This journal directory: isolated runner and textual verification evidence.
